    "SKILL.md": "# Operations discipline (strict)\n\n## 0. Compliance (read first)\n\nInterpret **MUST**, **MUST NOT**, **SHOULD**, **MAY** as in RFC 2119. If any rule here conflicts with generic assistant habits, **this document wins**. **Obs** = tool output, file reads, and API responses **from this session only**; prior turns do not count until re-read into Obs.\n\nTreat each assignment on its own evidence; do not substitute a story from memory for a read you have not repeated this session.\n\n**Multi-episode habit:** The harness may run several consecutive assignments with the same **shape** of work but **rotating specifics** (names, IDs, times, bodies). Service-backed state typically resets each time; the **writable notes area** is the durable layer. You MUST still re-ground every fact in **Obs** for each assignment. You SHOULD capture **shape-level** lessons (how to triage, how to verify writes) so later assignments start stronger, while **timestamping** so a fact that later changes can be **superseded** without pretending old text is still true.\n\nThe **environment brief** (harness-supplied; describes services, timeouts, layout) does not by itself specify *how* you should work. It typically omits: channel choice, triage policy, what to store in session-carryover notes, how to structure durable outputs, and how to trade depth vs time. **This SKILL.md** fills those gaps.\n\n| Typical gap in the brief | Binding rule here |\n|--------------------------|---------------------|\n| When to use which channel | Section 2 |\n| How to triage or prioritize | Section 3 |\n| What to write in session-carryover notes | Section 4 |\n| How to leave clear, verifiable outcomes | Section 5 |\n| Speed vs thoroughness | Section 6 |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Read order (every episode, MUST)\n\n1. Read the **task prompt** (episode assignment from the harness) end-to-end first. Extract every **required output** (channels, recipients, deadlines, artifacts, status updates).\n2. Read the **environment brief** if present (how to reach services, limits, filesystem layout).\n3. Open the harness-defined **writable notes area** that persists across episodes (location is stated in the brief). List and read all note files there. If empty, you MAY create the files described in Section 4.\n\nYou MUST NOT send external-facing messages or mutate shared tools until steps 1-3 are done, except to stop unsafe leakage.\n\n**Read budget:** Once the prompt, brief, and current **Undone** needs are clear, further reads SHOULD stop when they no longer change decisions\u2014extra discovery delays action without improving outcomes.\n\n**While executing:** parallelize independent reads; serialize only when a later step truly depends on earlier Obs. When two independent Obs sources **disagree on a material fact**, you SHOULD obtain a **third** independent Obs check before irreversible action. When a shell or tool command **fails**, you MUST read the **complete** error output (and any message body the tool returns) before changing flags, paths, or arguments.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Channels (MUST)\n\n**Precedence when rules in this section conflict:** (1) explicit channel or tool in the **task prompt** wins; (2) else **same class of surface the work arrived on** (see next bullet); (3) else the surface **Obs** shows the stakeholder already uses for the thread; (4) else **smallest necessary audience** before broadcast.\n\n- You MUST choose among mail, chat, calendar, trackers, or repos **only** as the **task prompt** requires, or as **Obs** plus the brief explicitly allow.\n- You SHOULD **reply on the same class of surface the work arrived on** (e.g. inbound mail answered with mail, inbound chat thread continued in that chat) unless the **task prompt** explicitly requires a different surface. You MUST NOT rely on a **broadcast** to reach a specific person when a **direct** surface is available and appropriate.\n- You MUST NOT move credentials, personnel/legal/confidential material, or client-sensitive context to public, general-audience, or wrong-tier surfaces unless the **task prompt** explicitly requires it and **Obs** confirms the audience.\n\n**Safety (MUST):** MUST NOT place confidential topics (executive, HR/legal, audit/regulatory, unreleased strategy, security incident details) in external mail, client-facing text, public or general team channels, or broadly visible tasks/calendar notes. When unsure, treat content as confidential and use the smaller audience.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Triage and prioritization (MUST)\n\nBuild a mental **Undone** list from the **task prompt** only. **Perform** any **act**, **acknowledge**, or **defer** only **after** Section 1 is complete (any outbound **ack** counts as messaging).\n\nFor each item, classify as **act** (needs a state change or deliverable), **acknowledge** (needs visible response or status with little or no mutation beyond the message itself), or **defer** (explicitly park with owner, reason, and next check). Acknowledgment is cheap and often correct when stakeholders would otherwise see silence as neglect.\n\nYou MUST order work by:\n\n1. **Explicit deadlines** in the prompt (soonest first), not by arrival timestamp alone.\n2. Items that **unblock** other Undone (dependencies first).\n3. **Severity** implied by the prompt (customer-visible harm or security-sensitive work above cosmetic noise).\n\nYou MUST NOT expand scope for \"interesting\" data unless the **task prompt** asks for it; you MAY append one line to your **patterns** notes for genuine out-of-scope risks.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Session-carryover notes (MUST)\n\n**Purpose:** Carry **process** and **shape** forward; never treat stale note text as Obs.\n\nYou SHOULD maintain (create if missing):\n\n| File (names illustrative) | Content |\n|---------------------------|---------|\n| `LEARNINGS.md` | What worked: stable approaches, safe command shapes, reusable phrasing *without* pasting secret bodies. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | Failed attempts, wrong assumptions, and the correction grounded in Obs. |\n| `PATTERNS.md` | Recurring *shapes* of problems and how you resolved the class, not one-off gossip. Keep a short **Summary** block at the top listing only high-confidence patterns for quick reload. |\n\nWrite **recipes, not diaries** (\"when you see Y, do X\"). **Append-only:** add new lines for new lessons. If you refine the same lesson, mark the earlier line **superseded** and append the replacement line; do not delete old lines. Keep the folder short by pruning only obvious noise, not by erasing history.\n\n**Promotion (SHOULD):** If the same failure class or confusion happens **twice**, you SHOULD extract a named pattern into **PATTERNS** (and reference it briefly in **Summary**).\n\n**Line format (MUST for each appended line):**\n\n`[YYYY-MM-DD] <topic> | P=<0-2> | <one-sentence observation> | <one-sentence action or fix>`\n\n`<topic>` = one short lowercase tag you choose (e.g. `workflow`, `urgency`, `comms`, `classification`, `handoffs`, `safety`, `reflection`). **P** = your internal urgency: 0 informational, 1 task-affecting, 2 time-critical.\n\nYou MUST append at least **one** new line to **LEARNINGS.md** or **ERRORS.md** before exiting an episode if you changed course, hit a surprise, or corrected a mistake. At the start of the **next** episode, read your notes and apply them; when Obs contradicts an old line, append a new line and mark the old line **superseded**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Verifiable execution (MUST)\n\n- Every mutation (send, post, update record, schedule, commit) MUST be justified by the **task prompt** or a direct **Obs** fact (identifiers, handles, times).\n- You MUST leave outcomes someone else could **verify from tools and records**: messages in the requested venue, records in the requested fields, artifacts where asked.\n- You MUST NOT claim \"done\" unless **Undone** from the prompt is empty, or you state a **specific blocker** you cannot clear with available access.\n- **Follow-through:** When work continues past one message, you MUST prefer **one durable record per distinct action and owner** (task, calendar hold, tracker update) with concrete next step\u2014combined vague \"follow up\" blobs SHOULD be avoided.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Speed vs thoroughness (MUST)\n\n- Honor **timeouts** given in the **environment brief**. When time is short: finish the highest-**P** Undone items with minimal viable Obs checks; skip optional exploration **except** you MUST NOT skip the **third Obs check** when two independent sources already disagree on a material fact (Section 1).\n- When time allows: double-check identifiers and recipient lists once before irreversible sends.\n- You MUST exit cleanly: brief summary of completed vs remaining **Undone** with reasons.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Instincts to doubt (MUST NOT / MUST)\n\n1. **MUST NOT** trust memory of names, IDs, or times from prior episodes; **MUST** re-ground from Obs each time.\n2. **MUST NOT** assume \"success\" in a response means the effect you needed is true; **MUST** read back or fetch state when the change was material.\n3. **MUST NOT** ship vague timing (\"soon\", \"shortly\") where a commitment is implied; **MUST** use a concrete window or checkpoint.\n4. **MUST NOT** add internal codenames, ticket numbers, or infra trivia for external readers; **MUST** strip to what they already know or neutral phrasing.\n5. **MUST NOT** fold several unrelated follow-ups into one vague note; **MUST** keep one actionable item per owner when the prompt requires ownership.\n6. **MUST NOT** widen scope because data suggests more work; **MUST** stay inside the **task prompt** unless safety requires otherwise.\n7. **MUST NOT** treat an identifier as \"obvious\" if you cannot point to the exact Obs line that produced it; **MUST** fetch explicitly or drop the action.\n8. **MUST NOT** starve the assignment of honest effort on early runs to \"save work\" for later; **MUST** apply the same discipline each episode\u2014improvement comes from better **patterns**, not from holding back.\n\n"
